  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows upper impulse lifting out of northeast KS \nand into eastern NE. Area of showers and storms tied to this feature \nare also lifting into eastern NE/NW MO. Another area of showers\nand storms is moving out of the TX Panhandle with this area of \nconvection associated with the broader mid/upper trough slowly \nmoving out of the Rockies and across the Plains. \n\nShort term models look to have a good handle on the precip coming \nout of the TX Panhandle and track it through south central KS this \nmorning and into southeast KS late this morning and early this \nafternoon. There will be a chance for a few storms to develop over \nwestern OK later this afternoon along a weak surface trough and \nwhere afternoon heating will be maximized. Some of this activity may \nwork into south central KS around 00z Sun, but confidence is low on \nhow much coverage there will be. Will go ahead and keep some small \npops in across southeast KS later tonight as the mean trough takes \nits time moving through. \n\nOutside of a few showers/storms Sun morning across southeast KS, \nmajority of the area still looks dry for Sun as brief upper ridging \nmoves into the area. By 12z Mon, there is still good model agreement \nin a strong upper low digging across central/southern CA with upper \nridging keeping things dry across the Plains for Mon. Low level jet \nwill ramp-up and veer across the Southern Plains Mon night \nsetting up some decent 850-700mb moisture transport and \nassociated elevated convection.\n\nUpper low will be digging across the Desert Southwest for Tue into \nTue night and at the same time, what is left of Polo will get \npulled in to this system and will rapidly lift northeast and \nwill be moving out into the southern Plains by Wed afternoon. \nThis will all lead to continued high rain chances for Tue \nthrough Wed. Extremely high PW values, around 200% of normal, \nare anticipated as this system starts so impact the area, which\nshould lead to very high rainfall rates and potential flooding \nas the main threat. Models start to diverge after Wed, with the \nGFS more progressive with the low compared to the ECMWF. \nHowever, regardless of which one verifies, high rainfall chances\nwill remain through at least Thursday",
